{"cuisine/cuisine-of-pittsburgh":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-pittsburgh","n":"Cuisine of Pittsburgh","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The culture of Pittsburgh stems from the city's long history as a center for cultural philanthropy, as well as its rich ethnic traditions.  In the 19th and 20th centuries, wealthy businessmen such as Andrew Carnegie, Henry J. Heinz, Henry Clay Frick, and nonprofit organizations such as the Carnegie Foundation donated millions of dollars to create educational and cultural institutions."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-pittsburgh"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Pittsburgh"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/haitian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/haitian-cuisine","n":"Haitian cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Haiti","intro":[["Haitian cuisine is a Creole cuisine that originates from a blend of several culinary styles that populated the western portion of the island of Hispaniola, namely African, French, Indigenous, Spanish, and Levantine influences. Haitian cuisine has some similarities with  \"criollo\" (Spanish for 'creole') cooking and similar to other Caribbean cuisine, but differs in several ways from its regional counterparts. Flavors are bold and spicy, demonstrating African and French influences, with notable derivatives coming from Indigenous and Spanish techniques."],["Levantine influences have made their way into the mainstream culture, due to an Arab migration over the years forming a community of shared Arab descent. Years of adaptation have led to these cuisines to merge into Haitian cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"haitian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haitian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"A Taste of Haiti","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Mirta Yurnet-Thomas","note":"Haitian home recipes with background on the country's food culture.","url":"https://www.hippocrenebooks.com/store/p463/A_Taste_of_Haiti.html","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780781814133"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Haitian patty","dish/haitian-patty"],["Le Soleil (restaurant)","dish/le-soleil-restaurant"],["Soup joumou","dish/soup-joumou"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Calabaza","ingredient/calabaza"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ee/Ayiti%26DR_2007_085.jpg/500px-Ayiti%26DR_2007_085.jpg","w":500,"h":328,"by":"Coolsarahs at English Wikipedia","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayiti%26DR_2007_085.jpg"}},"cuisine/northern-irish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/northern-irish-cuisine","n":"Northern Irish cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United Kingdom","intro":[["Northern Irish cuisine encompasses the cooking styles, traditions and recipes associated with Northern Ireland. It has distinctive attributes of its own, but has also drawn heavily from Irish and British cuisines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"northern-irish-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Irish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Paula McIntyre's Down to Earth Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Paula McIntyre","note":"Recipes built on Northern Irish produce by a Northern Irish chef and broadcaster.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781780730967","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781780730967"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/f/f7/St_George%27s_Market%2C_Belfast_%2810%29_-_geograph.org.uk_-_735666.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Ross","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_George's_Market%2C_Belfast_(10)_-_geograph.org.uk_-_735666.jpg"}},"dish/bagharj":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bagharj","n":"Bagharj","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Armenian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Bagharj is a traditional Armenian bread with both ritual and everyday uses. Depending on the recipe, it can be either salty, unsalted, or sweet. In Armenian folk culture, bagharj is attributed with symbolic and magical meanings."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bagharj"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bagharj"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Armenian cuisine","cuisine/armenian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Armenian Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thearmeniankitchen.com","note":"Armenian-American blog of family recipes and reviews of Armenian cookbooks.","url":"https://www.thearmeniankitchen.com/classic-armenian-recipes-another/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Armenian cuisine","links":[["Alani (dried fruit)","dish/alani-dried-fruit"],["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["İmam bayıldı","dish/imam-bay-ld"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Khorovats","dish/khorovats"],["Lavash","dish/lavash"],["Matnakash","dish/matnakash"],["Mikado cake","dish/mikado-cake"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Pirozhki","dish/pirozhki"],["Spas (soup)","dish/spas-soup"],["Tjvjik (food)","dish/tjvjik-food"]]}]},"ingredient/cream-cheese":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cream-cheese","n":"Cream cheese","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 7 dishes across 8 cuisines","intro":[["Cream cheese is a soft, usually mild-tasting fresh cheese made from milk and cream.  Cream cheese is not naturally matured and is meant to be consumed fresh, so it differs from other soft cheeses, such as Brie and Neufchâtel. It is more comparable in taste, texture, and production methods to Boursin and mascarpone. Stabilizers, such as carob bean gum and carrageenan, are often added in industrial production. It can also come in several flavors."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cream-cheese"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cream_cheese"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":8,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Strawberry delight","dish/strawberry-delight"]]],["Basque cuisine","cuisine/basque-cuisine",[["Basque cheesecake","dish/basque-cheesecake"]]],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Japanese cheesecake","dish/japanese-cheesecake"]]],["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",[["Chile con queso","dish/chile-con-queso"]]],["Minnesotan cuisine","cuisine/minnesotan-cuisine",[["Minnesota sushi","dish/minnesota-sushi"]]],["Pacific Northwest cuisine","cuisine/pacific-northwest-cuisine",[["Seattle-style hot dog","dish/seattle-style-hot-dog"]]],["Tex-Mex cuisine","cuisine/tex-mex-cuisine",[["Chile con queso","dish/chile-con-queso"]]],["Western American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-western-united-states",[["Seattle-style hot dog","dish/seattle-style-hot-dog"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Monterey Jack","ingredient/monterey-jack"],["Processed cheese","ingredient/processed-cheese"],["Velveeta","ingredient/velveeta"],["Clam","ingredient/clam"],["Sour cream","ingredient/sour-cream"],["Ham","ingredient/ham"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"],["Bialy","ingredient/bialy"],["Bialy (bread)","ingredient/bialy-bread"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f7/Philly_cream_cheese.jpg/500px-Philly_cream_cheese.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Renee Comet (photographer)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philly_cream_cheese.jpg"}},"ingredient/long-beans":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/long-beans","n":"Long beans","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The asparagus bean (Vigna unguiculata subsp. sesquipedalis) is a legume cultivated for its edible green pods containing immature seeds, like the green bean. It is also known as yardlong bean, pea bean, long-podded cowpea, Chinese long bean, snake bean, bodi, bora, and kosbanti (kousenband). Despite the common name of \"yardlong\", the pods are actually only about half a yard long, so the subspecies name sesquipedalis (one-and-a-half-foot-long; 1.5 feet (0.50 yd)) is a more accurate approximation."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-long-beans"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_beans"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Bamboo shoot","ingredient/bamboo-shoot"],["Bean curd","ingredient/bean-curd"],["Bumbu (seasoning)","ingredient/bumbu-seasoning"],["Caraway seed","ingredient/caraway-seed"],["Carrots","ingredient/carrots"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Dried shrimp","ingredient/dried-shrimp"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Leafy greens","ingredient/leafy-greens"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Onions","ingredient/onions"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5e/Vigna_unguiculata_Blanco2.286-cropped.jpg/500px-Vigna_unguiculata_Blanco2.286-cropped.jpg","w":500,"h":714,"by":"Francisco Manuel Blanco (O.S.A.)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vigna_unguiculata_Blanco2.286-cropped.jpg"}},"dish/jinju-bibimbap":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/jinju-bibimbap","n":"Jinju bibimbap","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from South Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Bibimbap, sometimes romanised as bi bim bap or bi bim bop, is a Korean rice dish. It is served as a bowl of warm white rice topped with namul (sautéed or blanched seasoned vegetables) and gochujang (chili pepper paste). Egg and sliced meat (usually beef) are common additions, stirred together thoroughly just before eating. The term bibim means \"mixing\" and bap is cooked rice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-jinju-bibimbap"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jinju_bibimbap"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Korean Bapsang","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from South Korean cuisine","links":[["Chalbori-ppang","dish/chalbori-ppang"],["Chimaek","dish/chimaek"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dak-galbi","dish/dak-galbi"],["Gilgeori toast","dish/gilgeori-toast"],["Hodu-gwaja","dish/hodu-gwaja"],["Hwangnam-ppang","dish/hwangnam-ppang"],["Jjolmyeon","dish/jjolmyeon"],["Kkulppang","dish/kkulppang"],["Milmyeon","dish/milmyeon"],["Rabokki","dish/rabokki"],["So-tteok-so-tteok","dish/so-tteok-so-tteok"],["Tornado potato","dish/tornado-potato"],["Ttongppang","dish/ttongppang"],["Yangnyeom chicken","dish/yangnyeom-chicken"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/60/Korean_cuisine-Jinju_bibimbap-01.jpg/500px-Korean_cuisine-Jinju_bibimbap-01.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"by shizu k (shezzz) at Flickr","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean_cuisine-Jinju_bibimbap-01.jpg"}},"ingredient/falukorv":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/falukorv","n":"Falukorv","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Falukorv, or Falu sausage in English, is a type of sausage (korv in Swedish) that originates from Falun, Sweden. It is made from a mixture of smoked pork and beef or veal, blended with potato starch flour, onion, salt, and mild spices. Falukorv is pre-cooked, which means it can be eaten cold without any further preparation."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-falukorv"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falukorv"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine",[["Korv stroganoff","dish/korv-stroganoff"]]]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/54/Falukorv.jpg/500px-Falukorv.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Mario Mlynek","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Falukorv.jpg"}},"ingredient/fried-egg":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/fried-egg","n":"Fried egg","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["A fried egg is a dish made from one or more eggs which are removed from their shells, placed into a frying pan and cooked. They are traditionally eaten for breakfast or brunch in many countries but may also be served at other times of the day."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-fried-egg"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fried_egg"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Cambodian Chinese cuisine","cuisine/cambodian-chinese-cuisine",[["Lort cha","dish/lort-cha"]]],["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine",[["Uitsmijter","dish/uitsmijter"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bean sprouts","ingredient/bean-sprouts"],["Chives","ingredient/chives"],["Dark soy sauce","ingredient/dark-soy-sauce"],["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Scallions","ingredient/scallions"],["Silver needle noodles","ingredient/silver-needle-noodles"],["Gouda","ingredient/gouda"],["Ham","ingredient/ham"],["White bread","ingredient/white-bread"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f0/Fried_Egg_2.jpg/500px-Fried_Egg_2.jpg","w":500,"h":310,"by":"Matthew Murdoch","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fried_Egg_2.jpg"}},"dish/casabe":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/casabe","n":"Casabe","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A round bread of ",["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"],", usually bitter cassava, which helps it keep. The root is peeled, grated and dried, the flour is sifted, and it's cooked on a clay or iron griddle, often over a wood fire. It's a daily staple in much of the Caribbean and Central America, and in 2024 UNESCO recognized the cassava bread of Cuba, the Dominican Republic, Haiti, Honduras and Venezuela, with its Indigenous and African roots. ",["Garifuna","cuisine/garifuna-foodways"]," women call it ereba and spend days making it to share."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"UNESCO, Traditional knowledge and practices for the making and consumption of cassava bread","url":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/traditional-knowledge-and-practices-for-the-making-and-consumption-of-cassava-bread-02118"},{"name":"Remezcla, New York City's Garifuna community preserves its rich culture through food","url":"https://remezcla.com/features/culture/garifuna-food-culture-new-york-city/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-casabe"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casabe"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Taíno heritage foodways","cuisine/taino-heritage-foodways"],["Garifuna foodways","cuisine/garifuna-foodways"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/91/Tortasdecasabe.JPG/500px-Tortasdecasabe.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Rosa García","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tortasdecasabe.JPG"}},"dish/kanuchi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/kanuchi","n":"Kanuchi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Cherokee foodways","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A ",["Cherokee","cuisine/cherokee-foodways"]," soup made from hickory nuts. The nuts are dried near the stove for a month or more, cracked, and pounded shell and all into soft balls that hold together with their own oil, which are kept frozen until it's time to cook."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Cherokee Phoenix, Hickory nuts mean kanuchi","url":"https://www.cherokeephoenix.org/health/hickory-nuts-mean-kanuchi/article_fdebe40b-9b48-5849-bf6a-1601baaecbc7.html"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-kanuchi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanuchi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cherokee foodways","cuisine/cherokee-foodways"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Cherokee Tribal Food Distribution Program","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"food.ebci-nsn.gov","note":"Traditional recipes from the Eastern Band of Cherokee Indians, like Three Sisters stew and chestnut bread.","url":"https://food.ebci-nsn.gov/?p=4673","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Cherokee foodways","links":[["Frybread","dish/frybread"]]}]},"dish/tejate":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/tejate","n":"Tejate","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Zapotec foodways","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A cold drink of ",["nixtamalized","ingredient/nixtamalization"]," corn, toasted ",["cacao","ingredient/theobroma-cacao"],", mamey seeds and rosita de cacao flowers, ground to a dough and worked by hand with water until pale curds float on top. It's made mostly by ",["Zapotec","cuisine/zapotec-foodways"]," women in Oaxaca's Central Valleys and served in painted gourd bowls, with as much syrup as you like."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Roads & Kingdoms, A hundred ladies stirring chocolate drinks","url":"https://roadsandkingdoms.com/2016/hundred-ladies-stirring-chocolate-drinks/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-tejate"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tejate"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Zapotec foodways","cuisine/zapotec-foodways"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Theobroma cacao","ingredient/theobroma-cacao"]]},{"h":"More from Zapotec foodways","links":[["Mole","dish/mole-sauce"]]},{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Masa","ingredient/masa"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/23/MX_MR_MUESTRA_ALIMENTARIA_IND%C